    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rabbit Hash earned its name from a 19th‑century rabbit‑hash meal, a story that began with a Christmas dinner debate in 1847.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit_Hash%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s quirky tradition of canine mayors began in 1998 when Goofy Borneman‑Calhoun, an adopted dog, was inaugurated for a four‑year term.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit_Hash%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Built in 1831, the Rabbit Hash General Store is Kentucky’s best‑preserved country store and was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1989.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        6,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit_Hash%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After a devastating fire in 2016, the community restored the General Store, reopening it on April 1 2017 with original materials and donor lumber.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit_Hash%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Rabbit Hash Historic District, covering 330 acres with 12 buildings, was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 2003.",
